    dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>;
    dcterms:description "Most 7th-century B.C. Assyrian inscriptions record that Nineveh had eight south- and east-facing gates: the Aššur Gate was the second of these. The Neo-Assyrian king Sennacherib constructed it and gave it the Akkadian ceremonial names Lilbur-iššak-Aššur (\"May the Vice-Regent of the God Aššur Endure\") and Libūr-iššak-Aššur (\"May the Vice-Regent of the God Aššur Stay in Good Health\").";
    dcterms:modified "2026-02-12T12:28:13-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE";
    dcterms:title "Aššur Gate";
